# Garbage collection needs a record a CALLER builds

**DONE.** A store collects its own garbage. `GCOptions` is a class a
caller builds, `GCResults` is one libstore made, and `GCAction` is the
word between them - which closes the last entry in `tasks/070`'s
queue.

The two answers below were both partly wrong, and the section "What it
actually took" at the end says how.

Originally: `GCAction` is the last vocabulary in `tasks/070`'s queue
that has a binding waiting for it, and the binding is not a method
with a few parameters. Upstream:

    virtual void collectGarbage(const GCOptions & options,
                                GCResults & results) = 0;

Two records, and only one of them is a shape this repo has.

## What the DSL does not have yet

`GCResults` is easy: two fields, and it is something libstore MADE,
which is exactly `@produced(by=...)`.

    struct GCResults {
        StringSet paths;      // roots, or what was/would be deleted
        uint64_t bytesFreed;
    };

`GCOptions` is the new thing. A caller BUILDS one:

    struct GCOptions {
        GCAction action{gcDeleteDead};
        bool ignoreLiveness{false};
        StorePathSet pathsToDelete;   // for gcDeleteSpecific
        uint64_t maxFreed{max};
    };

Every record in this repo today is produced. `PathInfo`,
`KeyedBuildResult`, `Realisation` - libstore made each one and Python
reads it. Nothing yet is a struct Python fills in and hands DOWN.

## The two answers, and the first is not obviously wrong

**Flatten it into keyword parameters.**

    def collect_garbage(self, action: GCAction = GCAction.DELETE_DEAD,
                        ignore_liveness: Bint = False,
                        paths_to_delete: "list[StorePath]" = None,
                        max_freed: U64 = ...) -> "GCResults":

The DSL needs nothing new: these are four parameters of types it
already spells, and the `Cxx` body fills the struct. It reads well
from Python - `store.collect_garbage(max_freed=1 << 30)` - and the
struct never reaches a caller.

Against it: `max_freed`'s default is
`std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max()`, which has no Python literal a
`default_source` would accept, so the default has to be spelled some
other way. And every future options struct repeats the flattening by
hand in a `Cxx` body, which is the mapping this repo exists to
derive.

**Teach the declaration an INPUT record.** A class whose fields
Python sets and whose C++ the emitter fills, the mirror of
`@produced`. More machinery, and it earns itself the second time an
options struct appears - `BuildOptions`, `SubstitutablePathInfo`'s
callers, and the fetch settings are candidates.

Do not decide this from GC alone. Count the input structs upstream
actually has before building the general answer.

### The count, taken 2026-09-02

Every `const X{Options,Settings,Params,Config,Request} &` parameter in
`src/libstore/include/nix/store/*.hh`:

    86  const StoreDirConfig &
    13  const ExperimentalFeatureSettings &
     5  const FileTransferRequest &
     4  const GCOptions &
     2  const WorkerSettings &
     2  const NarInfoDiskCacheSettings &
     2  const LocalSettings &
     1  const LogFileSettings &
     1  const GCSettings &
     1  const FileTransferSettings &
     1  const DerivationOptions &

Most of that is not input. `StoreDirConfig` is the store's own config
passed as CONTEXT - the store has one and a caller never builds one -
and every `*Settings` is global configuration, which `tasks/062`
already found is not reachable from a store URI. The honest
caller-built count is three: `GCOptions`, `FileTransferRequest`,
`DerivationOptions`.

So the shape does earn itself a second time, and not soon.

### Carl decided the rule, 2026-09-02

> Generally all classes and structs in C++ should be classes in
> Python.

Which settles it regardless of the count, and for a reason the count
does not carry: flattening makes the emitter invent a signature
upstream does not have, and `maxFreed`'s default is
`std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max()` - a value this repo would then
restate. As a class the defaults stay upstream's.

One carve-out, and it points the same way: where PYTHON already names
the concept, use Python's name. `std::chrono::microseconds` is
`datetime.timedelta`, `std::filesystem::path` is `pathlib.Path`, an
enum is a vocabulary. The rule is that a struct naming a concept
Python has no word for becomes a class, and `GCOptions` has none.

## The name clash, which is not cosmetic

`huggorm.collect_garbage` already EXISTS and means something else. It
is the boehm collector for the evaluator - `decl/eval.py`, a free
function over `huggorm::gc_collect` - and it collects Values, not
store paths.

A `Store.collect_garbage` beside it is two unrelated collectors one
import apart. A caller who reads "collect_garbage" in a traceback
will not know which. Options: name the store one after what upstream
calls the operation (`nix-store --gc`, so `gc` or `delete_garbage`),
or rename the evaluator's to say whose heap it sweeps
(`collect_value_garbage`). The second is a rename of a published
name, so it is Carl's call.

## Why it is worth doing

`nix-collect-garbage` is one of the two things a person does to a
store that this binding cannot do at all (the other is
`Store.repair_path`). It is also the only remaining USER of a queued
vocabulary, so `tasks/070` cannot finish without it.

## What it actually took

The second answer won, and it cost far less than this file expected.

**"More machinery" was wrong.** `GCOptions` needed none. `DrvOutput`
and `Signature` are already wire values a caller BUILDS - a declared
`__init__` with a `Cxx` body - and defaulted constructor parameters
already worked, vocabulary-typed fields included. The declaration
emitted whole on the first try. The mirror of `@produced` was never
needed, because a constructible wire value already IS one.

**One thing refused, and only under compile.** A derived `_from_parts`
hands its parts to the C++ constructor, and a `list[T]` part arrives
as a vector:

    gc.cpp:154:35: error: could not convert 'paths' from
    'std::vector<std::string>' to 'nix::StringSet'

The emitter knows `as_set`. It learns the TYPE from
`@binding(collection=...)` on the ELEMENT class, and the element here
is `str` - a builtin, with no class to state it on. So `@reads(member,
collection=...)` states it per field, and the field's answer wins over
the class's. One new keyword, one line of emitted diff.

That change paid for itself immediately. `MissingPaths` carried a
declared `_from_parts` whose whole body was three
`as_set<nix::StorePathSet>` calls - a mapping, hand-written - and the
derived one now emits exactly those. The body is deleted.

**`max_freed` takes None.** Upstream's default is
`std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max()`, which has no Python literal.
The constructor writes `nix::GCOptions{}` and assigns only what was
passed, so an absence leaves the member alone and the default stays
upstream's. It reads back as the number, because that is what the
struct holds.

**`GCResults.paths` is a StringSet, not a StorePathSet.** A root can
be a path outside the store, so the type cannot promise more than a
string. Read from the header rather than assumed from the name.

**And the name is `nix::GcStore`, not `GCStore`.** The source checkout
this repo reads for reference spells it one way and the packaged
2.34.8 it BUILDS against spells it the other. Every other name in the
header matches, which is what made it easy to miss:

    store.cpp:407: error: 'GCStore' in namespace 'nix' does not name
                          a type; did you mean 'GcStore'?

## What the tests found, which the reading would not have

**A path this process just added is NOT garbage.** The first test was
written asserting the opposite - nothing points at it, so it is dead -
and the collector refuted it: it ran, printed "finding garbage
collector roots... deleting garbage...", and returned no paths and
zero bytes.

`LocalStore::addToStore` calls `addTempRoot` on what it adds, and a
temp root belongs to the PROCESS. So the interpreter running the suite
holds every path it added, for as long as it runs, and a second
`Store` object on the same root changes nothing - the root is the
process's, not the object's.

That is the protection working, so it became the gate rather than
something to work around.

**And `ignore_liveness` does not get past it.** That was the next
guess and it was also wrong - `DELETE_SPECIFIC` with the flag set
raised:

    Cannot delete path '...-doomed' since it is still alive. To find
    out why, use: nix-store --query --roots and --referrers

`gc.cc:520` guards `findRootsNoTemp` with the flag. "NoTemp" - it
drops the PERMANENT roots, and the temporary ones are read
unconditionally on the lines after. So no field of GCOptions can
collect a path a process is holding, which is right: another process
might be part-way through adding it.

The consequence for the suite is that **`bytes_freed` has no hermetic
case at all**. A chroot store starts empty and everything the run adds
is held by the run. The hermetic gates are the refusal and the
protection; a live test asks the ambient store for `RETURN_DEAD` and
checks the answer is populated. The DELETING arm stays unproven on
purpose - a test must not collect a developer's store - and
`tasks/037` holds the question of what a live test gets to assume.

**The suite already refuses an unproven wire value.**
`test_every_wire_value_survives_its_own_round_trip` compares the
manifest's wire values against a hand-written SAMPLES table and fails
on any it cannot build. Adding these two without samples failed there.
Nothing had to be written for that; it was already right.

## What is still open

The name clash, which is Carl's and is not blocking. There is no
import collision - `Store.collect_garbage` is a method and
`huggorm.collect_garbage` is a free function - so the two coexist.
The cost is only that a traceback does not say which heap was swept.

Opened 2026-09-02 while landing `TrustedFlag`, because the queue in
070 said "GCAction" as if it were one more vocabulary and it is not.
Closed the same day.
